# Claude Code Configuration

This file provides Claude Code with comprehensive workflow instructions for both creative writing (Novel-OS) and software development (Agent-OS).

## System Detection and Workflow Selection

Claude Code supports two distinct workflow systems:

1. **Novel-OS**: Creative writing workflows (located in `~/.novel-os/`)
2. **Agent-OS**: Software development workflows (located in `~/.agent-os/`)

### Automatic System Detection

When a user types a command, Claude Code automatically detects which system to use:

- **Novel-OS commands**: `/plan-novel`, `/create-outline`, `/write-scenes`, `/analyze-manuscript`
- **Agent-OS commands**: `/plan-product`, `/analyze-product`, `/create-spec`, `/execute-tasks`

## Novel-OS (Creative Writing System)

### Novel-OS Structure
```
~/.novel-os/
├── standards/                  # Writing standards and style guides
│   ├── writing-style.md        # General writing style guide
│   ├── narrative-techniques.md # Narrative technique guidelines
│   └── genre-guides/           # Genre-specific writing guides
│       ├── literary-fiction.md
│       ├── mystery-thriller.md
│       └── fantasy-sci-fi.md
└── instructions/               # Novel-OS workflow instructions
    └── core/                   # Core writing workflow processes
        ├── plan-novel.md       # Novel planning and setup
        ├── create-outline.md   # Chapter/scene outline creation
        ├── write-scenes.md     # Scene writing workflows
        ├── write-scene.md      # Individual scene writing
        └── analyze-manuscript.md # Manuscript analysis
```

### Novel-OS Commands

Users can invoke Novel-OS workflows directly using these commands:

### Direct Command Usage
```
/plan-novel          → Execute novel planning workflow
/create-outline      → Create detailed chapter/scene outlines
/write-scenes        → Begin scene writing with guided workflows
/analyze-manuscript  → Analyze existing manuscript content
```

### Novel-OS Command Processing
When a user types a Novel-OS command (e.g., `/plan-novel`):

1. **Read the command file** from `~/.claude/commands/[command-name].md`
2. **Execute the corresponding workflow** from `~/.novel-os/instructions/core/[command-name].md`
3. **Apply writing standards** from `~/.novel-os/standards/` as needed
4. **Follow the complete workflow** as defined in the instruction files

### Novel-OS Command Examples
```
User types: /plan-novel
→ Read: ~/.claude/commands/plan-novel.md
→ Execute: ~/.novel-os/instructions/core/plan-novel.md

User types: /create-outline
→ Read: ~/.claude/commands/create-outline.md  
→ Execute: ~/.novel-os/instructions/core/create-outline.md

User types: /write-scenes
→ Read: ~/.claude/commands/write-scenes.md
→ Execute: ~/.novel-os/instructions/core/write-scenes.md

User types: /analyze-manuscript
→ Read: ~/.claude/commands/analyze-manuscript.md
→ Execute: ~/.novel-os/instructions/core/analyze-manuscript.md
```

## Agent-OS (Software Development System)

### Agent-OS Structure
```
~/.agent-os/
├── standards/                  # Development standards and style guides
│   ├── best-practices.md       # Core development best practices
│   ├── code-style.md          # General code style guide  
│   ├── tech-stack.md          # Technology stack specifications
│   └── code-style/            # Language-specific style guides
│       ├── css-style.md
│       ├── database-style.md
│       ├── documentation-style.md
│       ├── git_versioning-style.md
│       ├── html_jsx-style.md
│       ├── javascript-style.md
│       ├── node-style.md
│       ├── react-style.md
│       ├── testing-style.md
│       └── typescript-style.md
├── commands/                   # Direct user commands
│   ├── analyze-product.md      # Command: /analyze-product
│   ├── create-spec.md          # Command: /create-spec
│   ├── execute-tasks.md        # Command: /execute-tasks
│   └── plan-product.md         # Command: /plan-product
└── instructions/               # Agent OS workflow instructions
    └── core/                   # Core workflow processes
        ├── analyze-product.md  # Analyze existing codebase and install Agent OS
        ├── create-spec.md      # Create feature specifications
        ├── execute-task.md     # Execute individual tasks
        ├── execute-tasks.md    # Execute multiple tasks for a spec
        └── plan-product.md     # Initial product planning and setup
```

### Agent-OS Commands

Users can invoke Agent-OS workflows directly using these commands:

### Direct Command Usage
```
/plan-product        → Execute product planning workflow
/analyze-product     → Analyze existing codebase and install Agent OS  
/create-spec         → Create feature specifications
/execute-tasks       → Execute multiple tasks for a spec
```

### Agent-OS Command Processing
When a user types an Agent-OS command (e.g., `/plan-product`):

1. **Read the command file** from `~/.agent-os/commands/[command-name].md`
2. **Execute the corresponding workflow** from `~/.agent-os/instructions/core/[command-name].md`
3. **Apply development standards** from `~/.agent-os/standards/` during implementation
4. **Follow the complete workflow** as defined in the instruction files

### Agent-OS Command Examples
```
User types: /plan-product
→ Read: ~/.agent-os/commands/plan-product.md
→ Execute: ~/.agent-os/instructions/core/plan-product.md

User types: /create-spec
→ Read: ~/.agent-os/commands/create-spec.md  
→ Execute: ~/.agent-os/instructions/core/create-spec.md

User types: /analyze-product
→ Read: ~/.agent-os/commands/analyze-product.md
→ Execute: ~/.agent-os/instructions/core/analyze-product.md

User types: /execute-tasks
→ Read: ~/.agent-os/commands/execute-tasks.md
→ Execute: ~/.agent-os/instructions/core/execute-tasks.md
```

